# Line endings are LF in the working tree on EVERY platform.
#
# Without this the repo had no EOL policy at all, so what landed in the index
# depended on the committer's core.autocrlf and what a checkout produced
# depended on the checker-outer's. That is invisible until something compares
# RENDERED bytes to ON-DISK bytes — which bee does in three places
# (`dev render-skill-trees`, `dev render-hook-manifests`, the prompt-skew
# check). The renderers emit `\n`; a CRLF working tree makes all three
# disagree, and the 2.0.1 release proved it: green on ubuntu, twenty failures
# on windows-latest with `left: "{\r\n…"` against `right: "{\n…"`.
#
# `eol=lf` (not just `text=auto`) is the point: it pins the WORKING TREE, not
# only the index, so a Windows checkout and a Linux checkout hand the
# renderers identical bytes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries and images must never be touched by the rule above.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.exe binary

# bee log tier — append-only event logs that must union-merge across parallel
# branches/worktrees instead of producing trivial conflicts (P30 / P44 D3+D5,
# worktree-feature-parallelism). git's built-in `union` merge driver keeps both
# sides' appended lines; bee's readers/replay dedup by event id, so interleaved
# duplicates fold cleanly (see .bee/bin/lib/worktree-store.mjs replayLog).
.bee/decisions.jsonl merge=union
.bee/backlog.jsonl merge=union
.bee/review-candidates.jsonl merge=union
